Introduction
Financial returns do not behave the same way throughout a history. A market that trends smoothly for months may move into a highly volatile phase after an announcement, and the mean and variance of its returns can shift with it. The task in this article is to divide a return history into segments with different mean and variance, in a repeatable way, and to ask at which points the modeled behavior of returns changed enough that treating them as one continuous regime stops being a good fit.
The solution combines a Gaussian segment cost, a fixed penalty per change point, and the Pruned Exact Linear Time (PELT) algorithm, implemented as four cooperating MQL5 classes. PELT finds the optimal segmentation exactly, discarding candidate break points once they can no longer be optimal, provided a condition on the cost holds, which the script checks before it prunes.
The limitations matter enough to state at the start:
The method is retrospective. It marks up history and is not a trading signal. Because each segment must be at least a minimum length, it cannot report a change point in the last few bars, and change points near the end of the window can shift when new data arrives.
It is not a hypothesis test. "Statistical" here means derived from a Gaussian mean-and-variance segmentation model with a given penalty. There is no p-value or confidence level behind any individual break.
Repeatable does not mean assumption-free. The same data and settings always give the same answer, but the cost function, the penalty and the minimum segment length are choices, and different choices give different segmentations.
Understanding Change Point Detection
Time series
.
Here it is the sequence of logarithmic returns computed from historical closing prices, ordered by time. Detection is applied to returns, not to raw prices.
Structural break (change point)
. Throughout this article, a structural break, or change point, means a point where the segmentation model prefers to start a new segment, because a Gaussian with its own mean and variance on each side fits the data better than a single Gaussian would, by more than the penalty. This is a statement about the model's objective. It is not a claim that something happened in the market, and a real market event may or may not produce one.
Segmentation
. Once one or more change points are found, the time series is naturally divided into contiguous segments. Each segment is modeled as homogeneous, that is, generated by a single, unchanging return distribution.
The method looks for changes in the mean return, the variance, or both. These changes can accompany news events, crises and recovery periods. However, a detected boundary only indicates an improvement in the quality of the split under the selected model. It does not identify the cause of the change.
The Mathematics Behind Change Point Detection
Segmenting a time series
Suppose we have returns y_1, ..., y_n. A segmentation is a set of change points 0 = t_0 < t_1 < ... < t_m = n that splits the series into m contiguous segments. The cost function C(s, e) scores how badly a single Gaussian, with its own mean and variance, fits the returns between indices s and e. Pooling two different regimes into one segment inflates the variance and the cost.
C(s, e) = n * ln(var)
Here var is the maximum-likelihood variance of the segment: the sum of squared deviations from the segment mean divided by n, not n - 1. The cost is -2 ln L of the fitted Gaussian with the constants dropped that are identical for every segmentation, since the total number of observations is fixed.
If every return in a segment is identical, the variance is zero and ln(0) diverges, so the cost needs a floor:
C(s, e) = n * ln(max(var, ε)),    ε =
10
⁻⁸ × var_all
var_all is the variance of the entire return series, so the floor scales with the data and the segmentation does not change under rescaling of the returns. The floor is part of the objective, so whenever we call the solution "exact" below, we mean exact for this floored objective. In practice ε only binds on near-constant segments, such as runs of identical closes.
Objective and recursion
The total cost of a segmentation is the sum of its segment costs plus a penalty β per change point. Minimizing it is the optimal partitioning problem, solved by dynamic programming:
Total cost = Σ C(t_(k-
1
), t_k) + (m -
1
