F(t) = min over τ < t of [F(τ) + C(τ, t) + β],   F(
0
) = -β
F(t) is the minimum total cost of segmenting the first t observations, and the τ that achieves each minimum is stored so the change points can be recovered by walking back from F(n). Setting F(0) = -β cancels the penalty that the recursion would otherwise charge for the first segment. A prefix shorter than one minimum segment length has no legal segmentation at all, and the implementation tracks this explicitly instead of forcing an answer.
Choosing the penalty
Adding change points always lowers the fit cost, in the same way that adding parameters to a regression always lowers the residual error, so a fixed penalty β per change point is needed. If β is too small the algorithm chases noise, and if it is too large genuine shifts are smoothed over.
The cost above is already on the -2 ln L scale. As an initial calibration we use a BIC-style penalty, β = 2·ln(n), which corresponds to two additional parameters per segment, the mean and the variance. It is applied automatically when InpPenalty is zero or negative. This is not a universal calibration: counting the break position as a parameter would push the multiplier toward 3, and other ways of accounting for data complexity and properties may require a larger penalty. Treat β as a setting to examine, which is what the sensitivity scan later in the article is for.
The PELT pruning rule
A direct implementation of the recursion is O(n²), because every endpoint t checks every earlier τ. PELT removes most of that work with one observation. If at endpoint t a candidate τ satisfies F(τ) + C(τ, t) > F(t), then τ can never be the best last change point for any later endpoint T:
F(τ) + C(τ, T) + β  ≥  F(τ) + C(τ, t) + C(t, T) + β  >  F(t) + C(t, T) + β
The first step relies on one property of the cost: splitting a segment never makes the total worse, C(τ, t) + C(t, T) ≤ C(τ, T). The unfloored Gaussian cost has that property, so τ can be discarded for good. Under the conditions in the PELT literature the expected running time is close to linear, though the worst case remains O(n²). Pruning never changes the answer, only the amount of work.
Minimum segment length and deferred removal.
With a minimum length m, t is only a legal last change point for endpoints T ≥ t + m. Between t and t + m, swapping τ for t would create a segment that is too short, so τ may still be the best choice. The fix is to postpone the removal: when τ first satisfies the inequality at t, we mark it and keep it active until endpoint t + m, then drop it. Dropping it immediately can return a segmentation that is slightly worse than the true optimum.
Randomized testing supports this.
We compared three pruning variants with a brute-force partitioner that tries every admissible τ: immediate removal, deferred removal, and deferred removal combined with the check described below. The test used 2,400 simulated series (fixed seed, lengths 40 to 200 returns, minimum segment lengths 2 to 15). Immediate pruning produced a different segmentation from brute force on 74 of them. Both deferred variants matched on all 2,400. On a 500-return series with a minimum length of 10, pruning reduced the number of segment cost evaluations from 116,412 to 33,153, with identical change points. The check switched pruning off on 532 of the 2,400 series, mostly those with runs of identical or near-identical closes, so on those series the solver ran the full recursion. The test setup is described in Appendix A.
A caveat for the floored cost.
The inequality holds exactly for the unfloored Gaussian cost, but it can fail once the ε floor binds. Take two adjacent segments of equal length k with the same mean, where the first is constant, so its variance is floored to ε, and the second has variance 2ε. Together they have variance ε, so the merged segment costs 2k·ln ε, while the two pieces cost k·ln ε + k·ln 2ε, which is larger by k·ln 2. Splitting made the total worse, so for the floored cost the pruning argument is not guaranteed in general. A matching result in randomized tests is not a proof, and a stress test with a binding floor shows why the check is needed: without it, deferred pruning gave a different segmentation from brute force on 30 of 29,814 series, while the guarded version never did.
The script therefore prunes only when it can verify a sufficient condition. Every segment in the pruning argument has at least m returns, so if no segment of m or more returns has a variance below ε, the floor never binds and the unfloored inequality applies. It is enough to check windows of exactly m returns: any longer segment can be cut into blocks of m to 2m - 1 returns, and a block's variance is more than half the variance of its first m returns. If the smallest m-window variance is at least 2ε, every admissible segment sits above the floor. If the check fails, for instance on a long run of identical closes, the solver runs the full recursion with no pruning, which is slower but exact for the floored objective by construction.
Minimum segment length
A segment containing a single return has zero variance, so its cost is clamped to the floor, and the optimizer finds that cutting the series into single-return pieces makes every segment look perfectly fitted. On five simulated series of 500 pure-noise returns with a minimum length of 1, the segmentation returned between 496 and 499 change points. The script therefore rejects any InpMinSegmentLength below 2, and we recommend 5 to 10. The default of 10 is inside that range, and as long as the pruning check above passes, it runs genuine PELT.
Designing the Change Point Detection Framework
With the theory in place, the implementation is organized into four cooperating classes, each with a single, clear responsibility:
Class
Responsibility
CChangePointModel
Downloads price history, computes log returns, and builds the cumulative statistics arrays used for fast segment evaluation.
CSegmentCost
Computes the Gaussian mean/variance cost C(s, e) for any candidate segment, using the cumulative statistics from CChangePointModel. It also reports through AllowsPruning() whether PELT pruning is valid for the data at hand.
CPELTAlgorithm
Implements the optimal partitioning recursion with PELT pruning, including deferred pruning for minimum segment lengths, and recovers the optimal set of change points.
CChangePointDetector
Orchestrates the three classes above, chooses a default penalty when none is supplied, prints the results, and can optionally draw the detected change points directly on the chart.
Two rules run through all of them. All memory allocations are checked: ArrayResize() is only ever called through the small GrowArray() helper below, so a failed allocation stops the run with a message. And invalid values are marked as DBL_MAX and excluded from calculations, while non-existent dynamic programming states are never replaced with placeholder values. A detector that quietly manufactures a value for an undefined case can produce a segmentation that looks fine but is subtly wrong.
