The state validity logic is the part that is easiest to get wrong. A tempting shortcut is to fall back to τ = 0 whenever no candidate yet satisfies t - τ ≥ minSegLen. With minSegLen = 10, at t = 5 there is no legal segmentation of the first five returns, but the shortcut would still compute a real-looking F[5] from an illegal 5-bar segment. That fabricated value would then sit in the table indistinguishable from a valid one, and could silently influence every later endpoint. Here a candidate competes only if F[tau] is defined, the segment cost is defined and the segment respects the minimum length. If none qualify, F[t] stays DBL_MAX, an honest statement that the first t returns cannot yet be legally segmented. For the same reason, t is added to the candidate set only if F[t] is defined.
The pruning test needs the same care. When τ meets the inequality at t, it is not removed. We record t + m in pruneAt[tau], keep τ active, and drop it on the first pass at or after that endpoint. The call to AllowsPruning() means a cost that cannot guarantee the inequality, including the floored Gaussian cost on data where the check in Init() fails, is simply never pruned.
If F[n] is still DBL_MAX at the end, no legal segmentation exists and the function fails with a message instead of backtracking through an undefined table. Otherwise the change points are recovered by walking back through cp[], with a check that every predecessor is a valid, strictly smaller index, and the list is reversed into chronological order.
Running the Detector
CChangePointDetector::Run() validates its inputs (a minimum segment length of at least 2, and at least 2 × minSegLen returns, reported with the actual and required counts), applies the automatic penalty of 2·ln(n) when none is supplied, initializes the three classes and runs PELT. PrintResults() prints each change point with the mean and variance of the segment before and after it, followed by a summary of every segment. The reported date is m_times[idx], the first return of the new segment, and not the last return of the segment that just ended.
DrawOnChart() draws a dashed vertical line and a small label at each change point. It draws only when the chart shows the same symbol and timeframe that were analyzed, removes the objects of any earlier run first, and only reads results that are already final, so drawing never affects detection.
The optional sensitivity scan re-runs detection while varying one setting at a time, using the automatic penalty (InpPenalty is ignored): the penalty multiplied by 0.5, 1, 2 and 3, the minimum segment length set to 5, 10 and 20, and the lookback set to 0.8, 1.0 and 1.2 times InpLookback. It prints one line per run with the penalty used and the number and dates of the change points. A change point that keeps appearing near the same date is robust to these choices, and one that appears in only a few is borderline.
