Three conclusions follow.
There is one short burst of volatility.
Segment 2 stands out. Its volatility is about 1.8 times that of the next-highest segment, and change points #1 and #2, only 16 returns apart, bracket it. The sturdier statement is that return volatility was sharply elevated for about three weeks. The return data alone cannot say whether the cause was a news release, a central bank event or a liquidity shock.
The changes in the mean are unconvincing.
Every change point here is mainly a change in variance, by factors of 2.9 to 4.8 between neighboring segments. No segment's mean is more than about 1.25 standard errors from zero (Segment 2 at 1.25, Segment 6 at 1.07, the others at about 0.5 or less), so sign changes in the mean column, such as positive in Segment 3 and negative in Segment 4, should not be read as shifts in direction.
Short segments carry the most uncertainty.
The relative error of a segment's volatility is roughly one over the square root of twice its number of returns. For Segment 2's 16 returns that is about 18%, with a standard error of the mean of about 0.0024. For Segment 6's 129 returns it is about 6% and 0.00025. A short segment can be a real detection, but its parameters are noisy.
One caution applies to all of this. These are the results of the penalized segmentation objective, and the variance estimates assume Gaussian returns, which real returns violate, as the simulation below shows. Because the still-forming bar is excluded and the penalty depends only on n, running the script again on the same history and inputs reproduces the same segmentation exactly.
How stable is this segmentation?
To see how much of this depends on the settings, I ran the sensitivity scan on the same EURUSD history. Dates are printed as yy.mm.dd, and a +N after the fifth date means that many more change points were found than are listed.
