These results are illustrative Monte Carlo estimates based on 150 simulated series per model. They demonstrate the sensitivity of the default settings to distributional assumptions and should not be interpreted as universal or precisely calibrated false-alarm rates.
On data that matches the model's assumptions, the false-alarm rate is modest though not negligible. On fat-tailed data the detector fires almost every time, because a handful of extreme returns look to a Gaussian cost like a change in variance. On GARCH data, the segments describe volatility clustering rather than structural events. Real return series have both features, and nothing in a single run can tell the two apart, so detected change points are candidates to be checked against the sensitivity scan, not established events.
Conclusion
We built a complete Change Point Detector in MQL5: a Gaussian mean/variance segment cost, a penalized dynamic programming objective, and PELT pruning with deferred removal, organized into four reusable classes. The solver finds the exact optimum of the floored Gaussian objective. It prunes only when a checkable condition guarantees the pruning inequality for the floored cost, and otherwise runs the full recursion, so the result is exact either way. The output is a dated segmentation of a return history, useful as a basis for regime-conditioned testing of a strategy, for studying whether position-sizing rules should depend on volatility regimes, or simply as a record of when the modeled volatility of an instrument changed.
The main limitations are the ones stated at the start. The method is retrospective and cannot report change points in the most recent bars. It is repeatable but not assumption-free: on EURUSD, doubling the penalty replaces the five change points with a single, different one. The Gaussian cost does not capture heavy tails, volatility clustering or serial correlation, and the simulation shows how readily the default settings find change points in fat-tailed data. The implementation's explicit checks make it reject bad input and invalid states, but they do not make the model statistically correct.
Natural directions for development include regime-conditioned strategy validation, rolling or online detection for near-real-time alerts, cost functions built for heavy tails or GARCH-style variance (checked against the pruning condition), and Bayesian Change Point Detection, which would replace the fixed penalty with a probabilistic treatment of how likely a break is at each point.
Appendix A: Test Setup for the Pruning Checks
The checks were run with the attached script pelt_pruning_test.py, a Python port of the cost function and the recursion in ChangePointDetector.mq5. The main test uses a master seed (20261109); the efficiency check and the stress test use seeds derived from it (+1 and +2), and the minimum-length demonstration uses seeds 1 to 5, so the same seeds reproduce the same series and results.
Objective and variants.
The objective is the floored Gaussian cost, C(s, e) = n·ln(max(var, ε)) with ε = 10⁻⁸ × var_all, and F(0) = −β. The reference is a brute-force dynamic program that tries every admissible τ and does no pruning. Three pruned variants are compared with it: immediate removal, deferred removal, and deferred removal guarded by the window check (smallest minSegLen-window variance at least 2ε), which is the variant the MQL5 script implements.
Matching criterion.
A variant matches the brute-force result when the lists of change point indices are identical and the total objective F(n) agrees within 10⁻⁹ relative (10⁻⁷ absolute) tolerance.
Main test.
2,400 series, 400 of each of six types, generated through closing prices rounded to 5 decimal places (3 for the coarse-grid type): independent Gaussian returns (standard deviation 0.006); Student-t returns with 3 degrees of freedom (scale 0.004); GARCH(1,1) (ω = 2·10⁻⁷, α = 0.08, β = 0.90); random regime changes (segments of 10 to 60 returns, with random mean and volatility); Gaussian returns with one to three runs of identical closes; and small moves on a coarse price grid. Each series has 40 to 200 returns, a minimum segment length from 2 to 15, and a penalty of 0.5, 1 or 2 times 2·ln(n).
Floor-binding stress test.
29,814 series of 20 to 70 returns built from flat runs, tiny noise and ordinary noise, with minimum segment lengths from 2 to 6, so that the variance floor binds often.
Efficiency and minimum-length checks.
The cost evaluation counts come from one 500-return regime-change series with a minimum length of 10 and the automatic penalty. The Python port evaluates each candidate cost once per endpoint, whereas the MQL5 listing recomputes it in the candidate-set pass, so evaluation counts taken from the MQL5 code are higher than those reported here. The minimum-length demonstration uses five 500-return Gaussian series with the automatic penalty.
Results.
Immediate pruning differed from brute force on 74 of the 2,400 series. Deferred pruning, guarded or not, matched on all 2,400. The guard switched pruning off on 532 of them. In the stress test, deferred pruning without the guard differed on 30 of 29,814 series and the guarded version on none. These results come from the Python port, not from the MQL5 file itself.
